# XBD 12.1 Utility Argument Syntax and 12.2 Utility Syntax Guidelines
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# 12.2 says the standard utilities that claim conformance to the guidelines
# conform to all of them as if "should" read "shall" (almost every utility
# does, in its OPTIONS section). The guidelines are exercised through a
# representative set (cat, cut, sort, head, tail, wc, ls, sed, grep, mkdir,
# rm, pr and the getopts utility). A utility that the OPTIONS section
# excepts from a guideline is never used for that guideline (sort and
# Guideline 9; tail and a leading plus sign). Guidelines 1 to 3, 7 and 8 and
# the notation of the SYNOPSIS lines concern how utilities are named and
# specified, not what a running system does; see the .cov files.
# Everything runs in the POSIX locale.

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;5
class      required
since      1992
says       Options that take no option-argument can be grouped behind one
           hyphen-minus, followed by at most one option that takes an
           option-argument (given as the next argument), and the effect is
           that of giving the options one by one: cut -sf, sort -nrk, grep
           -ine, sed -ne, ls -ld, wc -lw, mkdir -pm, rm -rf and the getopts
           utility.
test       t/001-grouping.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;6
class      required
since      1992
says       An option and its option-argument can be given as two separate
           arguments (cut -f 1, sort -k 1,1, head -n 1, tail -n 1, grep -e
           pattern, sed -e script, mkdir -m 755).
test       t/002-separate-arguments.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;9
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.1/Ellipses
class      required
since      1992
says       Options given first are followed by any number of operands, and
           each operand is used: cat, grep (with file name prefixes), wc, head
           (with headers), ls, rm, mkdir and sed given several operands after
           their options.
test       t/003-several-operands.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.004
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;10
class      required
since      1992
says       The first -- that is not an option-argument ends the options: the
           arguments after it are operands even when they begin with a
           hyphen-minus, so files named -n, -x and -d are read, listed,
           created or removed as files by cat, wc, head, tail, grep, sort,
           cut, ls, mkdir, rm and sed, and a pattern or script after -- is
           taken from the next argument.
test       t/004-double-dash-ends-options.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.005
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;10
class      required
since      1992
says       A -- that is the option-argument of an option is that option's
           value and not the end of the options: grep -e -- uses the pattern
           --, and the getopts utility returns -- as the OPTARG of an option
           that takes an argument and goes on to the next option.
test       t/005-double-dash-as-option-argument.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.006
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;10
class      required
since      1992
says       Only the first -- ends the options: a second -- after it is an
           operand (cat -- -- reads a file named --, wc -l -- -- counts it,
           ls -- -- lists it).
test       t/006-second-double-dash-is-operand.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.007
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;14
class      required
since      1992
says       An argument that can be identified as an option, or as a group of
           options, is treated as such even when a file of that name exists
           in the working directory: with files named -u, -d and -l present,
           cat -u still reads standard input, ls -d still lists dot and wc -l
           still reads standard input.
test       t/007-option-looking-argument.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.008
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;11
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.1
class      required
since      1992
says       The order of different options relative to one another does not
           change the result (ls -l -d and -d -l, wc -w -l and -l -w, grep -n
           -i and -i -n, sort -n -r and -r -n, cut -s -f and -f -s).
test       t/008-option-order.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.009
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;11
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.1/Ellipses
class      required
since      1992
says       When an option that takes an option-argument is repeated, the
           option and option-argument pairs are used in command-line order:
           two sed -e scripts apply one after the other (swapping them gives
           another result), grep -e patterns together select the lines that
           match any of them, and the getopts utility returns the repeated
           option-arguments in the order given.
test       t/009-repeated-option-order.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.010
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;13
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2
class      required
since      1992
says       With a file named - present, the operand - means standard input
           for the utilities whose OPERANDS say so (cat, cut, sort), and for
           head, tail, grep and sed it means standard input or the file named -
           (which of the two is implementation-defined, as their OPERANDS
           say), never something else.
note       The choice for those four utilities is implementation-defined and
           must be documented; the test t/010-dash-operand.sh accepts either and does not
           check the documentation.
test       t/010-dash-operand.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.011
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2
class      required
since      2001
says       A file operand and an option-argument can contain bytes outside the
           portable character set: cat reads a file with such a byte in its
           name, and grep -e finds a line holding such a byte.
test       t/011-nonportable-bytes.sh

assertion  XBD.12.2-utility-syntax-guidelines.012
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;3
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;5
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;6
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap12.html#12.2/Guideline&nbsp;10
class      required
since      1992
says       The getopts utility follows the guidelines when it parses a mixed
           command line: grouped flags, an option-argument as the next
           argument or attached, the end of the options at -- and at the
           first operand, with OPTIND naming the first operand.
note       The getopts page puts these guidelines in its DESCRIPTION; the
           getopts module covers the pieces one by one, this assertion the
           combination.
test       t/012-getopts-guidelines.sh
